fact WorkItem(id: "WI-949", description: some(value: "SCALAND'S THREE SCAN PASSES EACH RE-SPELL THE SAME NAMESPACE/SORT RECURSION, and the pass-3 port made it three copies. `Loader.scanItemsPass1` / `scanItemsPass2` / `scanItemsPass3` each derive the child scope the same way — joinSegments -> makeQualified(prefix, shortName) -> look the scope symbol up -> recurse with the new (scopeTerm, prefix) — and differ ONLY in what they do at a rule / operation / import / requires item. FOUND by /code-review on the pass-3 port (2026-08-03). WHY IT MATTERS rather than being tidiness: the WI-853 top-level-import arm had to be threaded through two of them in lockstep, the `pending` buffer through two, and the three disagree TODAY on the miss case — pass 1 cannot miss (it defines), pass 2 uses `sortSym.foreach` (silent skip), pass 3 now reports (`descend`). A single `forEachScope(items, scopeTerm, prefix)(body)` walker parameterised by the per-item action would make the three passes differ only where they actually disagree, and give the miss ONE answer. NOT urgent and deliberately not folded into the pass-3 delivery: it touches all three passes at once, which is a different risk profile from adding one. ACCEPTANCE: the walker extracted, the three passes expressed through it, suite green — and the miss behaviour stated once."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T08:00:18Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-949", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-08-03 (58152bb7). ONE walker: `walkScopes(items, scopeTerm, prefix, pass)` owns the descent (joinSegments -> makeQualified -> obtain child scope -> recurse); a pass supplies `enterScope` (what it does AT a scope, yielding the scope to recurse into) and `atItem` (every other item). Four passes now: DefinePass / ImportPass / RuleHeadPass / LoadPass — the phase-2 `loadItems` was a FOURTH copy of the same recursion, so it joined rather than being left as the odd one out. `ScopeDecl` (a parameterized enum over Namespace | SortWithBody, forwarding name/imports/items) is what lets the DEFINING pass tell the two apart without an unreachable third arm — passing the bare `Item` would have needed a silent-skip or fabricated-error catch-all. THE MISS ANSWER, stated once, in `lookupDefined`: report. `lookupScope` is its scope-shaped use. FOLDED IN from /code-review: LoadPass's `EntityItem` arm was the SAME invariant (DefinePass defines every entity) and still dropped its `entity_of` fact on a `.foreach` miss — it now goes through the same helper. Pass 1 also lost a `fileTerms` parameter it threaded but never used. DRIVE + CONTROL: new LoaderTest case calls `Loader.load` WITHOUT `scanDefinitions` — the only reachable miss, since `loadAll` runs the defining pass over every file first — and asserts the diagnostic names the scope; VERIFIED discriminating by backing the report out (fails with an empty ArrayBuffer, and only that test fails). Control = `load namespace with scoping`, which takes the SAME hoisted fixture (`buildNamespacedParsedFile`) through the full pipeline and passes either way. Full scaland suite green, 307 tests. /code-review run (4 agents: reuse / simplification / efficiency / altitude). Efficiency clean (ScopeDecl+Some allocate only on the ~196 scope openers of the stdlib, x4 passes; the pass classes capture fewer slots per scope than the closures they replaced). Applied: parameterized-enum ScopeDecl (15 lines -> 3), test-fixture hoist, stale `scanItemsPass1` doc reference in parse/IR.scala, the EntityItem arm. SKIPPED, with reasons: (a) `kb.tryResolveSymbol` instead of the raw `byQualifiedName.get` — 8 other pre-existing sites in Loader.scala use the raw form, so changing only the new one makes the file LESS uniform; take it as a sweep or not at all. (b) making `lookupScope` the trait's DEFAULT `enterScope` so a fifth pass cannot re-open the divergence by returning None for its own reason — real hardening, but it needs `kb`/`errors` as ScopePass members and hands DefinePass an errors buffer it never writes; over-abstraction for two identical implementors. (c) NARROWING THE API so the miss is unrepresentable (make `load` private to `loadAll`, or have `scanDefinitions` return a token `load` requires) — this IS the deeper fix for the only reachable miss, and reporting is the floor rather than the ceiling; not taken because it is outside WI-949's scope and would delete the only drive of the loud miss. Worth its own ticket if the two-caller `load`/`scanDefinitions` split is ever revisited. FILED WI-953: the rustland twin — same four-way duplication (scan_items_pass1/2/3 + load_items over find_scope_by_name), and its pass 3 CANNOT report a miss at all, taking no errors vec.", at: "2026-08-03T08:00:21Z")
